# GE-T10's corpus (SMYSL-2.5 §5.5; TX-P8 step 4). Ten subjects from the history of medicine and
# chemistry, each named in the five tier-1 languages — one `x.text/entity` per spelling, every
# pair of spellings joined by `same-as` — and a dozen or so units about each. Every pair, because
# a strict class is A-12.4's greedy clique partition: a star of edges from one spelling joins it
# to one other and leaves the rest alone, which is what the first run of this harness did.
#
# WHO WROTE THIS. One person, by hand, together with the gold in `slots.tsv` and the questions
# in `questions.tsv`, and after the templates the harness measures had been written. That is
# the figure's first limit and it is not a small one: GE-T10 asks for *expert-filled* slots,
# and a gold set written by the hand that wrote the templates is a set whose difficulty that
# hand chose. What the gold can still do honestly is say what a reader asking the question
# would want in each slot, by the criteria written at the top of `slots.tsv`, without looking
# at what a template selects; where the two disagree the disagreement is the measurement.
# The facts are compressed and some are simplified; the corpus is a fixture, not a history.
#
# Each unit carries a comment naming the slots the gold puts it in, or `gold: none` with the
# reason. The cases a template is expected to get wrong are planted on purpose and marked
# PLANT, each with the failure it probes:
#
#   OQ-86    a rebuttal (or a cause, or an example) that itself names the subject, which the
#            edge slots' `except` removes and the kind slots then pick up
#   passing  a unit that names the subject in passing and is about something else
#   remainder a unit about the subject but not about what the question asked of it, which a
#            slot bound only to `$subject` cannot tell apart
#   depth    a dependent three `grounds` hops away, past `dependent`'s depth 2 (it missed: see mi/levy)
#   mode     a unit with a holder and no `text:mode`, which `!= reported` never matches
#   limit    a subject mentioned by more units than a slot's `limit 12`
#   morph    a question whose subject is inflected away from its entity's spelling (in
#            `questions.tsv`, not here)

@schema x.text/v1 { version: 1, types: ["x.text/entity"], relations: [x.text/mentions, x.text/same-as] }

# ============================================================================================
# 1. cholera — define, explain ("why does it spread so quickly"), trace
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/cholera { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ cholera

@x.text/entity e/cholera-es { status: speculative, lang: es, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:es" } }
~ cólera

@x.text/entity e/cholera-fr { status: speculative, lang: fr, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:fr" } }
~ choléra

@x.text/entity e/cholera-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ холера

# gold: definition
@definition ch/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/1" } }
~ Cholera is an acute intestinal infection that causes profuse watery diarrhoea.

# gold: definition (in Russian, through same-as)
@definition ch/def-ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/1r" } }
~ Холера — острая кишечная инфекция с сильной диареей.

# gold: characteristic
@claim ch/fast { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/2" } }
~ Cholera can kill within hours of the first symptoms.

# gold: characteristic, and the unit the explain question is about
@claim ch/water { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/3" } }
~ Cholera spreads through drinking water fouled with sewage.

# gold: revision (trace) — superseded by ch/water; a reader asking what cholera is wants the
# current account, so not characteristic
@claim ch/air { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/4" } }
~ Cholera spreads through foul air rising from filth and marshes.

# gold: characteristic
@finding ch/vibrio { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/5" } }
~ Koch isolated the comma bacillus from cholera patients in 1884.

# gold: exemplifies (define) — a case that does not name the disease
@observation ch/case { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/6" } }
~ A coal-heaver on Broad Street fell ill at dawn on 31 August and was dead by nightfall.

# PLANT OQ-86 (exemplifies). gold: exemplifies (define)
@observation ch/case-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/7" } }
~ A cholera patient in Soho died eleven hours after the first cramps.

# gold: counter (define, explain) — rebuts ch/water, does not name cholera
@claim ch/counter { status: speculative }
~ Many who drank from the Broad Street pump never fell ill at all.

# PLANT OQ-86 (counter). gold: counter (define) — rebuts ch/vibrio and names cholera
@claim ch/counter-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/8" } }
~ Pettenkofer drank a broth of cholera bacilli in 1892 and suffered only a mild diarrhoea.

# gold: none — rebuts the superseded ch/air, which is not counter-evidence about cholera now
@claim ch/counter-air { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/9" } }
~ The houses nearest the sewer vents were spared, so foul air was not the vehicle.

# gold: evidence (explain); PLANT remainder: also an observation the trace introduction slot
# will take, and it is not how the disease entered the record
@observation ch/outbreak { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/10" } }
~ The Soho outbreak of cholera in 1854 killed 616 people within a few streets of one pump.

# gold: cause (explain, trace) — causes ch/outbreak, does not name cholera
@claim ch/cause { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/11" } }
~ A cesspit leaking into the Broad Street well contaminated the water.

# PLANT OQ-86 (cause). gold: cause (explain, trace) — causes ch/intro and names cholera
@claim ch/cause-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/12" } }
~ Troops and pilgrims moving along the trade routes carried cholera out of Bengal.

# gold: introduction (trace)
@observation ch/intro { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/13" } }
~ Cholera first spread beyond the Ganges delta in 1817.

# gold: introduction (trace), in Russian
@prose ch/intro-ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/14" } }
~ Холера впервые пришла в Россию в 1823 году через Астрахань.

# gold: hypothesis (explain)
@hypothesis ch/hyp { status: speculative }
~ Cholera may be carried by a living animalcule that multiplies in water.

# gold: open-question (define, explain)
@question ch/oq { status: speculative }
~ Why did some cholera epidemics burn out before the water was ever cleaned?

# PLANT passing. gold: none — about the Board, not the disease
@claim ch/passing { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:cholera/15" } }
~ The Board of Health met weekly through the cholera year of 1849.

@rel ch/water --supersedes--> ch/air
@rel ch/case --exemplifies--> ch/fast
@rel ch/case-named --exemplifies--> ch/fast
@rel ch/counter --rebuts--> ch/water
@rel ch/counter-named --rebuts--> ch/vibrio
@rel ch/counter-air --rebuts--> ch/air
@rel ch/cause --causes--> ch/outbreak
@rel ch/cause-named --causes--> ch/intro

# A clique, not a star: a strict class (A-12.4) is a clique, so every pair is asserted.
@rel e/cholera --x.text/same-as--> e/cholera-es
@rel e/cholera --x.text/same-as--> e/cholera-fr
@rel e/cholera --x.text/same-as--> e/cholera-ru
@rel e/cholera-es --x.text/same-as--> e/cholera-fr
@rel e/cholera-es --x.text/same-as--> e/cholera-ru
@rel e/cholera-fr --x.text/same-as--> e/cholera-ru

@rel ch/def --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/def-ru --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera-ru
@rel ch/fast --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/water --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/air --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/vibrio --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/case-named --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/counter-named --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/outbreak --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/cause-named --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/intro --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/intro-ru --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera-ru
@rel ch/hyp --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/oq --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera
@rel ch/passing --x.text/mentions--> e/cholera

# ============================================================================================
# 2. scurvy — define, explain ("why do sailors get it"), verify ("cured by citrus fruit")
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/scurvy { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ scurvy

@x.text/entity e/scurvy-de { status: speculative, lang: de, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:de" } }
~ Skorbut

@x.text/entity e/scurvy-es { status: speculative, lang: es, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:es" } }
~ escorbuto

@x.text/entity e/scurvy-fr { status: speculative, lang: fr, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:fr" } }
~ scorbut

@x.text/entity e/scurvy-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ цинга

# gold: definition
@definition sc/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/1" } }
~ Scurvy is a disease of long deprivation of fresh food, marked by bleeding gums and old wounds reopening.

# gold: characteristic
@claim sc/gums { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/2" } }
~ Scurvy loosens the teeth and makes the gums swell and bleed.

# gold: characteristic; the claim the verify question asks about
@claim sc/citrus { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/3" } }
~ Scurvy is cured by oranges and lemons.

# gold: characteristic; cause-relevant to explain
@claim sc/voyage { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/4" } }
~ Scurvy struck crews on voyages longer than about ten weeks without fresh stores.

# gold: support, evidence (verify) — grounds sc/citrus-derived, does not name scurvy
@data sc/rations { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/5" } }
~ The Salisbury's sick men given two oranges and a lemon a day were fit for duty within six days.

# gold: characteristic; rests on sc/rations
@claim sc/citrus-derived { status: inferred, grounds: [sc/rations] }
~ Citrus fruit reverses scurvy within a week.

# gold: evidence (explain); not verify, which asks about citrus
@observation sc/anson { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/6" } }
~ Anson lost most of his men to scurvy on the voyage round the world of 1740 to 1744.

# PLANT remainder (evidence). gold: evidence (explain) only — about where scurvy struck, not
# about citrus
@observation sc/siege { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/7" } }
~ Scurvy broke out among the defenders in the winter of the siege.

# gold: counter (define, verify) — rebuts sc/citrus, does not name scurvy; not explain, which
# asks why sailors fell ill and not what cures them
@claim sc/counter { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/8" } }
~ Lime juice boiled in copper pans saved nobody on the Arctic expeditions of the 1870s.

# PLANT remainder (counter). gold: counter (define, explain); not verify — it rebuts the
# voyage claim, not the citrus cure
@claim sc/counter-voyage { status: speculative }
~ Some crews went sixteen weeks at sea on salt meat alone and stayed healthy.

# gold: hypothesis (explain)
@hypothesis sc/hyp { status: speculative }
~ Scurvy may come from the sea air itself rather than from what sailors eat.

# PLANT mode (reported, verify). gold: reported (verify)
@claim sc/reported { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/9" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "Lind" }
~ Lind wrote that oranges and lemons were the most effective remedy for scurvy at sea.

# PLANT remainder (reported). gold: none for verify — reported, but not about citrus
@claim sc/reported-air { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/10" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "the ship's surgeon" }
~ The ship's surgeon reported that scurvy came from the damp below decks.

# gold: open-question (define, explain)
@question sc/oq { status: speculative }
~ Why did scurvy return to the navy after 1860 when lime juice was still issued?

# gold: characteristic (in Russian)
@claim sc/gums-ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:scurvy/11" } }
~ При цинге кровоточат дёсны и расшатываются зубы.

@rel sc/counter --rebuts--> sc/citrus
@rel sc/counter-voyage --rebuts--> sc/voyage

# A clique, not a star: a strict class (A-12.4) is a clique, so every pair is asserted.
@rel e/scurvy --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-de
@rel e/scurvy --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-es
@rel e/scurvy --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-fr
@rel e/scurvy --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-ru
@rel e/scurvy-de --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-es
@rel e/scurvy-de --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-fr
@rel e/scurvy-de --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-ru
@rel e/scurvy-es --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-fr
@rel e/scurvy-es --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-ru
@rel e/scurvy-fr --x.text/same-as--> e/scurvy-ru

@rel sc/def --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/gums --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/citrus --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/voyage --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/citrus-derived --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/anson --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/siege --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/hyp --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/reported --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/reported-air --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/oq --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy
@rel sc/gums-ru --x.text/mentions--> e/scurvy-ru

# ============================================================================================
# 3. miasma — define, verify ("causes disease"), depend
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/miasma { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ miasma

@x.text/entity e/miasma-fr { status: speculative, lang: fr, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:fr" } }
~ miasme

@x.text/entity e/miasma-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ миазмы

# gold: definition
@definition mi/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/1" } }
~ Miasma is bad air arising from rotting matter, once held to be the cause of epidemic disease.

# gold: characteristic; the claim verify asks about
@claim mi/causes { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/2" } }
~ Miasma causes cholera, plague and fever.

# gold: characteristic
@claim mi/smell { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/3" } }
~ Miasma is known by its stench.

# gold: support (verify) — grounds mi/causes-derived, does not name miasma
@observation mi/marsh { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/4" } }
~ Fevers were commonest in the low marshy districts by the river.

# gold: characteristic; dependent (depend, depth 1)
@claim mi/causes-derived { status: inferred, grounds: [mi/marsh, mi/smell] }
~ The miasma of the marshes breeds intermittent fever.

# gold: dependent (depend, depth 2) — rests on mi/causes-derived, does not name miasma
@decision mi/drain { status: inferred, grounds: [mi/causes-derived] }
~ The parish resolved to drain the river meadows.

# gold: dependent (depend). Meant as PLANT depth — three `grounds` hops from mi/smell, past
# `dependent`'s depth 2 — and it probes nothing: the walk starts from every unit about the subject,
# mi/causes-derived among them, and from there this is two hops. Kept, and recorded as a plant that
# missed; a dependent past depth 2 needs a chain whose middle names nothing
@decision mi/levy { status: inferred, grounds: [mi/drain] }
~ A rate of a penny in the pound was levied to pay for the drains.

# gold: dependent (depend, depth 1) — rests on mi/smell, does not name miasma
@decision mi/sewer { status: inferred, grounds: [mi/smell] }
~ The Metropolitan Board built the intercepting sewers to carry the stench away from the Thames.

# gold: counter (define, verify) — rebuts mi/causes, does not name miasma
@claim mi/counter { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/5" } }
~ The workers at the sewage farms were no sicker than anybody else.

# PLANT OQ-86 (counter). gold: counter (define, verify) — rebuts mi/causes and names miasma
@claim mi/counter-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/6" } }
~ Snow argued that miasma could not explain why one household fell ill and its neighbour did not.

# gold: evidence (verify)
@observation mi/stench { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/7" } }
~ In the Great Stink of 1858 the miasma over the Thames brought no rise in deaths.

# gold: reported (verify)
@claim mi/reported { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/8" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "Chadwick" }
~ Chadwick told the commission that all smell is disease, meaning the miasma of the slums.

# gold: related (depend) — elaborates mi/def, does not name miasma
@prose mi/related { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/9" } }
~ The word comes from the Greek for pollution.

# gold: definition (French)
@definition mi/def-fr { status: cited, lang: fr, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:miasma/10" } }
~ Le miasme est une émanation malsaine des matières en putréfaction.

# gold: open-question (define)
@question mi/oq { status: speculative }
~ Why was miasma believed for so long after the evidence turned against it?

@rel mi/counter --rebuts--> mi/causes
@rel mi/counter-named --rebuts--> mi/causes
@rel mi/related --elaborates--> mi/def

# A clique, not a star: a strict class (A-12.4) is a clique, so every pair is asserted.
@rel e/miasma --x.text/same-as--> e/miasma-fr
@rel e/miasma --x.text/same-as--> e/miasma-ru
@rel e/miasma-fr --x.text/same-as--> e/miasma-ru

@rel mi/def --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/causes --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/smell --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/causes-derived --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/counter-named --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/stench --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/reported --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma
@rel mi/def-fr --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma-fr
@rel mi/oq --x.text/mentions--> e/miasma

# ============================================================================================
# 4. smallpox — define, trace ("where did it come from"), locate
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/smallpox { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ smallpox

@x.text/entity e/smallpox-de { status: speculative, lang: de, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:de" } }
~ Pocken

@x.text/entity e/smallpox-es { status: speculative, lang: es, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:es" } }
~ viruela

@x.text/entity e/smallpox-fr { status: speculative, lang: fr, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:fr" } }
~ variole

@x.text/entity e/smallpox-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ оспа

# PLANT limit: smallpox is mentioned by fifteen units, and `location` keeps twelve. Every
# mentioning unit is gold for location, because "where is it mentioned" asks for all of them.

# gold: definition; location
@definition sp/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/1" } }
~ Smallpox is a contagious viral disease marked by fever and a pustular rash that scars.

# gold: characteristic; location
@claim sp/deadly { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/2" } }
~ Smallpox killed about three in ten of those it infected.

# gold: characteristic; location
@claim sp/immunity { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/3" } }
~ A survivor of smallpox never caught it again.

# gold: characteristic; location
@finding sp/eradicated { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/4" } }
~ Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980.

# gold: introduction (trace); location
@observation sp/egypt { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/5" } }
~ The mummy of Ramesses V bears the scars of smallpox.

# gold: introduction (trace); location
@observation sp/china { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/6" } }
~ A Chinese text of the fourth century describes smallpox for the first time in writing.

# gold: revision (trace); location — superseded by sp/origin-new
@claim sp/origin-old { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/7" } }
~ Smallpox reached Europe with the returning Crusaders.

# gold: characteristic; location
@claim sp/origin-new { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/8" } }
~ Smallpox was in Europe centuries before the Crusades.

# gold: cause (trace) — causes sp/americas, does not name smallpox
@claim sp/cause { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/9" } }
~ The ships of the conquest carried sick sailors across the Atlantic.

# gold: introduction (trace); location
@observation sp/americas { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/10" } }
~ Smallpox reached the Americas in 1518 and emptied whole cities.

# gold: location
@observation sp/variolation { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/11" } }
~ Variolation against smallpox was practised in Constantinople before 1717.

# gold: location
@claim sp/montagu { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/12" }, "text:holder": "Lady Mary Wortley Montagu" }
~ Lady Mary Wortley Montagu had her son inoculated against smallpox.

# gold: location (German)
@claim sp/de { status: cited, lang: de, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/13" } }
~ Die Pocken wurden in Deutschland 1874 durch die Impfpflicht zurückgedrängt.

# gold: location (Spanish)
@claim sp/es { status: cited, lang: es, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/14" } }
~ La expedición Balmis llevó la vacuna contra la viruela a América en 1803.

# gold: location (Russian)
@prose sp/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/15" } }
~ Екатерина II привилась от оспы в 1768 году.

# gold: location; open-question (define)
@question sp/oq { status: speculative }
~ Could smallpox return from a laboratory stock?

# gold: related (locate) — elaborates sp/eradicated, does not name smallpox
@prose sp/related { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/16" } }
~ The last natural case was a hospital cook in Merca, Somalia, in 1977.

# gold: exemplifies (define) — exemplifies sp/deadly
@data sp/example { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:smallpox/17" } }
~ Of the 6,000 sick in Boston in 1721, 844 died.

@rel sp/origin-new --supersedes--> sp/origin-old
@rel sp/cause --causes--> sp/americas
@rel sp/related --elaborates--> sp/eradicated
@rel sp/example --exemplifies--> sp/deadly

# A clique, not a star: a strict class (A-12.4) is a clique, so every pair is asserted.
@rel e/smallpox --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-de
@rel e/smallpox --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-es
@rel e/smallpox --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-fr
@rel e/smallpox --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-ru
@rel e/smallpox-de --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-es
@rel e/smallpox-de --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-fr
@rel e/smallpox-de --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-ru
@rel e/smallpox-es --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-fr
@rel e/smallpox-es --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-ru
@rel e/smallpox-fr --x.text/same-as--> e/smallpox-ru

@rel sp/def --x.text/mentions--> e/smallpox
@rel sp/deadly --x.text/mentions--> e/smallpox
@rel sp/immunity --x.text/mentions--> e/smallpox
@rel sp/eradicated --x.text/mentions--> e/smallpox
@rel sp/egypt --x.text/mentions--> e/smallpox
