# ============================================================================================
# 5. Semmelweis — define, attribute ("who said he was mad"), verify ("cut deaths by washing")
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/semmelweis { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ Semmelweis

@x.text/entity e/semmelweis-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ Земмельвейс

# gold: definition
@definition se/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/1" } }
~ Semmelweis was a Hungarian obstetrician at the Vienna General Hospital in the 1840s.

# gold: characteristic; the claim verify asks about
@claim se/washing { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/2" } }
~ Semmelweis cut deaths from childbed fever by having students wash in chlorinated lime.

# gold: support (verify) — grounds se/washing-derived, does not name Semmelweis
@data se/rates { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/3" } }
~ Mortality in the first clinic fell from about ten per cent to under two after May 1847.

# gold: characteristic; rests on se/rates
@claim se/washing-derived { status: inferred, grounds: [se/rates] }
~ The fall in deaths at Semmelweis's clinic followed the handwashing order.

# gold: characteristic
@claim se/asylum { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/4" } }
~ Semmelweis died in an asylum in 1865.

# gold: reported (attribute); holder (attribute) — the claim the attribute question asks about
@claim se/mad { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/5" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "his colleagues" }
~ His colleagues said Semmelweis had gone mad.

# gold: reported (verify), holder (attribute) — reported; about the handwashing
@claim se/reported { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/6" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "Skoda" }
~ Skoda told the Academy that Semmelweis's washing had halved the deaths.

# gold: holder, stance (attribute) — asserted by a named holder
@claim se/stance { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/7" }, "text:mode": "asserted", "text:holder": "Semmelweis" }
~ Semmelweis wrote that the doctors themselves carried the particles to the women.

# PLANT mode. gold: holder, stance (attribute) — a holder and no mode
@claim se/stance-nomode { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/8" }, "text:holder": "Scanzoni" }
~ Scanzoni declared Semmelweis's doctrine of no use to obstetrics.

# gold: counter (define, verify) — rebuts se/washing, does not name Semmelweis
@claim se/counter { status: speculative }
~ Deaths fell in the Prague clinic in the same years without any washing order.

# gold: evidence (verify)
@observation se/clinics { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/9" } }
~ Semmelweis saw that the midwives' clinic lost far fewer mothers than the doctors' clinic.

# gold: exemplifies (define) — exemplifies se/washing
@observation se/example { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/10" } }
~ In April 1847 the first clinic lost 57 mothers; in August, after the order, it lost 6.

# gold: characteristic (Russian)
@claim se/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:semmelweis/11" } }
~ Земмельвейс ввёл мытьё рук хлорной известью в 1847 году.

@rel se/counter --rebuts--> se/washing
@rel se/example --exemplifies--> se/washing

@rel e/semmelweis --x.text/same-as--> e/semmelweis-ru

@rel se/def --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/washing --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/washing-derived --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/asylum --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/mad --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/reported --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/stance --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/stance-nomode --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/clinics --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis
@rel se/ru --x.text/mentions--> e/semmelweis-ru

# ============================================================================================
# 6. John Snow — define, attribute ("who said he was wrong"), explain ("why the pump handle")
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/snow { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ John Snow

@x.text/entity e/snow-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ Джон Сноу

# gold: definition
@definition sn/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/1" } }
~ John Snow was a London physician, a pioneer of anaesthesia and of epidemiology.

# gold: characteristic
@claim sn/map { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/2" } }
~ John Snow mapped the cholera deaths of 1854 around the Broad Street pump.

# gold: characteristic; the act the explain question asks about
@claim sn/handle { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/3" } }
~ John Snow persuaded the parish to take the handle off the Broad Street pump.

# gold: cause (explain) — causes sn/handle, does not name Snow
@claim sn/cause { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/4" } }
~ Nearly every death that week was in a household that drew water from the one pump.

# gold: characteristic
@claim sn/anaesthesia { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/5" } }
~ John Snow gave chloroform to Queen Victoria at the birth of Prince Leopold.

# gold: reported, holder (attribute) — the claim the attribute question asks about
@claim sn/wrong { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/6" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "the Board of Health" }
~ The Board of Health reported that John Snow's water theory was wrong.

# gold: holder, stance (attribute)
@claim sn/stance { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/7" }, "text:mode": "asserted", "text:holder": "John Snow" }
~ John Snow held that cholera is swallowed, not breathed.

# PLANT mode. gold: holder, stance (attribute) — a holder and no mode
@claim sn/lancet { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/8" }, "text:holder": "The Lancet" }
~ The Lancet dismissed John Snow's sewer theory as a fallacy.

# gold: evidence (explain)
@observation sn/workhouse { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/9" } }
~ John Snow found that the workhouse with its own well had almost no deaths.

# gold: counter (define, explain) — rebuts sn/handle, does not name Snow
@claim sn/counter { status: speculative }
~ The outbreak was already waning before the handle came off.

# gold: hypothesis (explain)
@hypothesis sn/hyp { status: speculative }
~ John Snow may have acted on the brewery workers who drank only beer and stayed well.

# PLANT passing. gold: none — about the pub, not the man
@claim sn/passing { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/10" } }
~ A public house on Broadwick Street is now named after John Snow.

# gold: characteristic (Russian)
@claim sn/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:snow/11" } }
~ Джон Сноу доказал, что холера передаётся через воду.

@rel sn/cause --causes--> sn/handle
@rel sn/counter --rebuts--> sn/handle

@rel e/snow --x.text/same-as--> e/snow-ru

@rel sn/def --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/map --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/handle --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/anaesthesia --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/wrong --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/stance --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/lancet --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/workhouse --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/hyp --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/passing --x.text/mentions--> e/snow
@rel sn/ru --x.text/mentions--> e/snow-ru

# ============================================================================================
# 7. Jenner — define, attribute ("who claimed he invented vaccination"), trace
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/jenner { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ Jenner

@x.text/entity e/jenner-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ Дженнер

# gold: definition
@definition je/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/1" } }
~ Jenner was an English country doctor who introduced vaccination with cowpox.

# gold: characteristic
@claim je/phipps { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/2" } }
~ Jenner inoculated James Phipps with cowpox in May 1796.

# gold: introduction (trace)
@observation je/milkmaids { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/3" } }
~ Jenner heard that milkmaids who had caught cowpox did not take smallpox.

# gold: revision (trace) — superseded by je/first-new
@claim je/first-old { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/4" } }
~ Jenner was the first to protect anyone with cowpox.

# gold: characteristic
@claim je/first-new { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/5" } }
~ Jenner was not the first to use cowpox, but the first to test it and publish.

# gold: cause (trace) — causes je/milkmaids, does not name Jenner
@claim je/cause { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/6" } }
~ Country lore in Gloucestershire held that cowpox kept the pox away.

# gold: reported, holder (attribute) — the claim the attribute question asks about
@claim je/invented { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/7" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "his biographer" }
~ His biographer claimed that Jenner invented vaccination alone.

# gold: holder, stance (attribute)
@claim je/stance { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/8" }, "text:mode": "asserted", "text:holder": "Jenner" }
~ Jenner maintained that cowpox gave lifelong protection.

# PLANT OQ-86 (counter). gold: counter (define) — rebuts je/stance and names Jenner
@claim je/counter-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/9" } }
~ Jenner's own patients were later found to need revaccination.

# gold: exemplifies (define) — exemplifies je/phipps
@observation je/example { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/10" } }
~ The boy was exposed to smallpox six weeks later and did not fall ill.

# gold: introduction (trace), Russian
@prose je/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:jenner/11" } }
~ Дженнер опубликовал свои наблюдения в 1798 году.

@rel je/first-new --supersedes--> je/first-old
@rel je/cause --causes--> je/milkmaids
@rel je/counter-named --rebuts--> je/stance
@rel je/example --exemplifies--> je/phipps

@rel e/jenner --x.text/same-as--> e/jenner-ru

@rel je/def --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/phipps --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/milkmaids --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/first-old --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/first-new --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/invented --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/stance --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/counter-named --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner
@rel je/ru --x.text/mentions--> e/jenner-ru

# ============================================================================================
# 8. James Lind — define, verify ("ran the first controlled trial"), attribute ("proved the cure")
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/lind { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ James Lind

@x.text/entity e/lind-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ Джеймс Линд

# gold: definition
@definition li/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/1" } }
~ James Lind was a Scottish naval surgeon who wrote A Treatise of the Scurvy in 1753.

# gold: characteristic; the claim verify asks about
@claim li/trial { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/2" } }
~ James Lind ran the first controlled trial in medicine aboard the Salisbury in 1747.

# gold: support (verify) — grounds li/trial-derived, does not name Lind
@data li/arms { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/3" } }
~ Twelve sick sailors were divided into six pairs, each pair given a different remedy.

# gold: characteristic
@claim li/trial-derived { status: inferred, grounds: [li/arms] }
~ James Lind's comparison of six remedies had the shape of a controlled trial.

# gold: counter (define, verify) — rebuts li/trial, does not name Lind
@claim li/counter { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/4" } }
~ Daniel's trial of pulse and water in the Book of Daniel compared two groups long before.

# PLANT remainder (counter). gold: counter (define); not verify — rebuts the cure, not the trial
@claim li/counter-cure { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/5" } }
~ The treatise recommended a boiled concentrate that had lost its power.

# gold: reported, holder (attribute) — the claim the attribute question asks about
@claim li/proved { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/6" }, "text:mode": "reported", "text:holder": "Blane" }
~ Blane said that James Lind had proved the cure for scurvy.

# gold: holder, stance (attribute)
@claim li/stance { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/7" }, "text:mode": "asserted", "text:holder": "James Lind" }
~ James Lind held that scurvy came from moist air as much as from diet.

# gold: evidence (verify)
@observation li/salisbury { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/8" } }
~ James Lind recorded that the two given oranges and lemons were fit for duty after six days.

# gold: characteristic (Russian)
@claim li/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lind/9" } }
~ Джеймс Линд сравнил шесть средств от цинги.

@rel li/counter --rebuts--> li/trial
@rel li/counter-cure --rebuts--> li/stance

@rel e/lind --x.text/same-as--> e/lind-ru

@rel li/def --x.text/mentions--> e/lind
@rel li/trial --x.text/mentions--> e/lind
@rel li/trial-derived --x.text/mentions--> e/lind
@rel li/proved --x.text/mentions--> e/lind
@rel li/stance --x.text/mentions--> e/lind
@rel li/salisbury --x.text/mentions--> e/lind
@rel li/ru --x.text/mentions--> e/lind-ru

# ============================================================================================
# 9. Joseph Lister — define, explain ("why carbolic acid"), locate
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/lister { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ Joseph Lister

@x.text/entity e/lister-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ Джозеф Листер

# gold: definition; location
@definition ls/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/1" } }
~ Joseph Lister was a British surgeon who introduced antiseptic surgery.

# gold: characteristic; location — the act the explain question asks about
@claim ls/carbolic { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/2" } }
~ Joseph Lister dressed wounds with carbolic acid from 1865.

# gold: cause (explain) — causes ls/carbolic, does not name Lister
@claim ls/cause { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/3" } }
~ Carbolic acid had been used to stop the stench of the Carlisle sewage fields.

# PLANT OQ-86 (cause). gold: cause (explain) — causes ls/carbolic and names Lister
@claim ls/cause-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/4" } }
~ Joseph Lister read Pasteur's papers on fermentation and took germs to cause putrefaction.

# gold: characteristic; location
@claim ls/mortality { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/5" } }
~ Joseph Lister's amputation deaths fell from about 45 to 15 in a hundred.

# gold: evidence (explain); location
@observation ls/ward { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/6" } }
~ Joseph Lister saw that compound fractures festered where simple fractures healed.

# gold: hypothesis (explain); location
@hypothesis ls/hyp { status: speculative }
~ Joseph Lister may have chosen carbolic because it was cheap and already to hand.

# gold: counter (define) — rebuts ls/mortality, does not name Lister; not explain, which asks why
# carbolic and not whether it worked
@claim ls/counter { status: speculative }
~ The fall in deaths owed as much to cleaner wards as to the spray.

# gold: open-question (define, explain); location
@question ls/oq { status: speculative }
~ Why did Joseph Lister give up the carbolic spray in 1890?

# gold: related (locate) — elaborates ls/carbolic, does not name Lister
@prose ls/related { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/7" } }
~ Carbolic acid is phenol, distilled from coal tar.

# gold: location (Russian)
@prose ls/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/8" } }
~ Джозеф Листер стал основоположником антисептики.

# PLANT passing. gold: location only — it is a mention, which is what locate asks for
@claim ls/passing { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:lister/9" } }
~ A brand of mouthwash was named after Joseph Lister in 1879.

@rel ls/cause --causes--> ls/carbolic
@rel ls/cause-named --causes--> ls/carbolic
@rel ls/counter --rebuts--> ls/mortality
@rel ls/related --elaborates--> ls/carbolic

@rel e/lister --x.text/same-as--> e/lister-ru

@rel ls/def --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/carbolic --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/cause-named --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/mortality --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/ward --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/hyp --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/oq --x.text/mentions--> e/lister
@rel ls/ru --x.text/mentions--> e/lister-ru
@rel ls/passing --x.text/mentions--> e/lister

# ============================================================================================
# 10. phlogiston — define, compare ("difference from oxygen"), depend
# ============================================================================================

@x.text/entity e/phlogiston { status: speculative, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:en" } }
~ phlogiston

@x.text/entity e/phlogiston-es { status: speculative, lang: es, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:es" } }
~ flogisto

@x.text/entity e/phlogiston-fr { status: speculative, lang: fr, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:fr" } }
~ phlogistique

@x.text/entity e/phlogiston-ru { status: speculative, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "alias:ru" } }
~ флогистон

# gold: definition
@definition ph/def { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/1" } }
~ Phlogiston was the principle of fire, thought to be released by every burning body.

# gold: characteristic
@claim ph/release { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/2" } }
~ A metal heated in air gives up its phlogiston and leaves a calx.

# gold: characteristic
@claim ph/negative { status: speculative }
~ Phlogiston was sometimes said to have negative weight.

# gold: contrast (compare) — contrasts ph/release, does not name phlogiston
@claim ph/contrast { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/3" } }
~ Lavoisier showed that a burning metal takes oxygen from the air and grows heavier.

# gold: counter (define, compare) — rebuts ph/release, does not name phlogiston
@data ph/counter { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/4" } }
~ Mercury calx weighs more than the mercury it was made from.

# PLANT OQ-86 (counter). gold: counter (define, compare) — rebuts ph/negative, names phlogiston
@claim ph/counter-named { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/5" } }
~ No one ever weighed phlogiston, so its weight was never negative or anything else.

# gold: dependent (depend, depth 1) — rests on ph/release, does not name phlogiston
@claim ph/dep1 { status: inferred, grounds: [ph/release] }
~ Dephlogisticated air is air that has taken up nothing from the flame.

# gold: dependent (depend, depth 2) — rests on ph/dep1
@claim ph/dep2 { status: inferred, grounds: [ph/dep1] }
~ Priestley named the gas he made from mercury calx dephlogisticated air.

# gold: open-question (define)
@question ph/oq { status: speculative }
~ Why did Priestley keep to phlogiston to the end of his life?

# gold: related (depend) — elaborates ph/def, does not name phlogiston
@prose ph/related { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/6" } }
~ Stahl took the theory from Becher's terra pinguis.

# gold: characteristic (Russian)
@claim ph/ru { status: cited, lang: ru, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/7" } }
~ Флогистон считали невесомой огненной материей.

# PLANT passing. gold: none — a history of the word, not of the substance
@claim ph/passing { status: cited, source: { kind: doc, ref: "hist:phlogiston/8" } }
~ The word phlogiston appears in a hundred and forty chemistry textbooks before 1800.

@rel ph/contrast --contrasts--> ph/release
@rel ph/counter --rebuts--> ph/release
@rel ph/counter-named --rebuts--> ph/negative
@rel ph/related --elaborates--> ph/def

# A clique, not a star: a strict class (A-12.4) is a clique, so every pair is asserted.
@rel e/phlogiston --x.text/same-as--> e/phlogiston-es
@rel e/phlogiston --x.text/same-as--> e/phlogiston-fr
@rel e/phlogiston --x.text/same-as--> e/phlogiston-ru
@rel e/phlogiston-es --x.text/same-as--> e/phlogiston-fr
@rel e/phlogiston-es --x.text/same-as--> e/phlogiston-ru
@rel e/phlogiston-fr --x.text/same-as--> e/phlogiston-ru

@rel ph/def --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston
@rel ph/release --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston
@rel ph/negative --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston
@rel ph/counter-named --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston
@rel ph/oq --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston
@rel ph/ru --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston-ru
@rel ph/passing --x.text/mentions--> e/phlogiston
